  "slug": "cmx-340",
  "title": "CMX-340",
  "year": "1976",
  "subtitle": "The broadcast edit console whose keyboard became an industry standard — with a jog knob box, the GIZMO, you could reposition for left or right hand",
  "why_scout_cares": "The museum's first broadcast video-editing control surface. The CMX-340 turned tape editing into a computerized, keyboard-driven ritual: the editor typed edit points into a dedicated function keyboard and scrubbed with a jog wheel. Its most embodied touch is the GIZMO — a jog-knob box integrated into the keyboard that could be re-positioned for left- or right-handed editors. Within 18 months, over 90% of broadcast videotape editing ran on CMX systems, and its keyboard style seeded Grass Valley, Calaway, and Strassner editing systems.",
